  "verdict": "REPLICATES. Both parts of the frozen claim hold on SWE100. No deployable arm reliably beats deadline_only at any cell, and the budget as a fraction of deadline utility is small at per-request scale (8.5%, 9.0%, 13.3% at 56/100/160 ms) and large at the campaign cells (62.5% at 3500, 82.0% at 5000). The negative result is corpus-robust across traces.",
  "stronger_evidence_than_either_corpus_alone": "The cells where an arm appears to win do NOT overlap between corpora. per_key_loo wins at 56 ms on SWE277 and LOSES there on SWE100; it wins at 100 and 160 on SWE100 and loses at both on SWE277. best_constant wins at 160 on SWE277 and loses there on SWE100. Zero cells agree. A real effect at some cell would replicate at that cell; scattered wins with no cross-corpus overlap are noise. This converts 8968434's 'probably noise, interval spans zero' into a positive demonstration.",
  "quantitative_difference_worth_noting": "The budget at kv=3500 is 62.5% of deadline utility on SWE100 against 88.9% on SWE277. The qualitative pattern is identical, large at campaign cells and small at per-request cells, but the magnitude is not stable, so budget fractions should be quoted as a range across corpora rather than a point.",
  "scope": "Both corpora share benchmark, scaffold and model, so this is replication across TRACES, not across WORKLOADS. It does not establish that the conclusion holds for non-SWE agents. SWE100 has 100 tasks and 4640 rows against SWE277's 277 tasks and 13410 rows.",
  "why_this_ran": "I had concluded no valuable offline experiment remained and was about to stop the loop, then found every result in the lane rested on one corpus while a second declared corpus sat unused. Replication had already caught a defective statistic once this run (c211b37).",
  "premise": "Forced eviction; with no memory pressure every arm scores zero; neither corpus can exhibit contention; hidden-swap milliseconds, not wall clock. The per-request kv values were derived from Fresh-277's context distribution and reused unchanged, so they are held fixed across corpora by construction rather than re-derived per corpus."
